Child bash (in both sub_agent and explore_agent) now blocks the same catastrophic commands as the parent, and refuses risky commands when the parent would have asked the user first. Committed as 0e9441bd. Build, all tests, clippy -D warnings and fmt --check pass.

What changed in `src/tools.rs`:
• **Hard deny list (layer 1):** the list is now one shared constant, `HARD_DENY_PATTERNS`, read through one shared function, `hard_deny_refusal`. The parent's default bash and the child both use it, so the child refuses rm -rf / and the rest in every approval state, with the parent's exact refusal text.
• **Commands the parent would ask about (layer 2):** in `child_confirm_refusal`, a command flagged by `analyze_bash_command` is refused when the parent has a confirm prompt. The refusal tells the model to let the parent run it. When the parent has no prompt (piped or --yes), the child allows it, just as the parent does, so the evolve loop's behaviour is unchanged.
• **Where the approval state comes from:** `!config.auto_approve`. This is the same bool `build_bash_tool` uses to decide whether the parent gets a prompt, and `main.rs` already folds "not interactive" into it. Both child builders pass it into sub_agent_child_tools as a new parent_confirms argument, so no extra plumbing was needed.
• **The child wrapper now always applies.** It used to be skipped when the user had no deny rules; now it checks hard deny, then the user's deny list, then the confirm rule.
• **The comment listing the child's layers** now states the rule as: no path that can run bash is more permissive than the parent, except layer 3.

One deliberate difference from the parent: the child is stricter in one case. It still refuses a flagged command after the user chose "always", or when one of their allow patterns would match. It is never looser.

Tests are in a new file, src/tools_child_bash_tests.rs, so tools.rs doesn't grow more than it has to:
• The child refuses rm -rf / in both approval states, matching the parent's text.
• A flagged command (git push --force) is refused when the parent confirms. When the parent doesn't, the harmless flagged command echo 'TRUNCATE TABLE …' is allowed and actually runs.
• `ls, cargo test and git status pass through in both states, each with a check that analyze_bash_command doesn't flag it. Only ls is actually executed, because running cargo test` would start the test suite inside itself.
• The parent's default deny list is compared against the shared constant.
• A weak source check confirms both builders pass the approval state; it doesn't prove the check fires.

I ran the positive control in one step: making the layer-2 check always allow failed exactly the two layer-2 tests by name. After restoring it, all tests passed and no NEUTERED marker remains.

Remaining gap: layer 3, detect_git_redirection_escape, is still not checked in the child. The parent only runs it when its working directory is pinned. I commented on #977 with what landed, the design decision and this gap, and left the issue open for it.

Other changes:
• `src/main.rs` registers the new test file.
• `src/tools_user_deny_tests.rs` passes the new argument, and one doc comment there was updated now that the child is always wrapped.
• The size-register entry for src/tools.rs in tests/module_size.rs is raised from 5033 to 5068 lines.
• ARCHITECTURE.md has a new tools.rs bullet, which marks the Day-216 "still not enforced" sentence and its "unwrapped when the list is empty" clause as superseded.
